  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.2. Strengthened capacity of enterprises to adopt new business models, technology and techniques to enhance productivity and sustainability</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">-  In 2020, a self-financed training modality have been adopted by national partner organizations (VCCI HCM, Hochiminh City Centre of Supporting Industries Development (CSID), and The Assistance Centre for SME (SME TAC) to market and conduct SME development training financed by SME fee  and/or state budget.


  - 409 policy makers and social partners trained on productivity, working conditions and SME policies. 
- Relevant aspects of ILO/Sustaining Competitive and Responsible Enterprises (SCORE) Training have been embedded in the existing public training programmes of VCCI HCM, CSID, Wood associations, SME TAC, Industrial Development Centre (IDC).
- 5 lead buyers promoted ILO/SCORE training in their supply chain.
- 1 lead buyer sponsored ILO/SCORE Training in their supply chain.
- 12 cases study were documented.
5 events to promote ILO/SCORE and supply chain development related topic to government, constituents and stakeholders were organized.
7 lead buyers requested specific meeting and information session on ILO/SCORE Training.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">4.2.1. Number of member States with effective interventions to support productivity, entrepreneurship, innovation and enterprise sustainability</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.4. Increased capacity of member States and enterprises to develop policies and measures that promote the alignment of business practices with decent work and a human-centred approach to the future of work</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">-  Increasing the number of regular public dialogues on the best way to practice the CSR  and social audit between the actors in supply chains (suppliers/ buyers(MNEs), Business Association, government, producer, and trade union), in particular, the Small and Medium Enterprises (SMEs) in the seafood and wood processing industries;
-The technical note was submitted to the Directorate of Fisheries by the 1st ever established national CSR Think Tank in Viet Nam, for policy formulation, revision, and enforcement for sustainable development of the seafood sector, especially in the context of Covid-19. This National Think Tank is promoting three key areas: dialogue, capacity building, and advocacy for CSR/RBC promotion in the seafood sector;
-For sustaining the result and replicating the good practices , the key elements of ILS and CSR/RBC were embedded as cross-cutting content in the extra-curriculum system of key universities, where a number of professors/teachers were trained as trainers. -ILO supported studies on CRS/RBC in the wood and seafood processing sectors and the reports were disseminated through a tripartite policy consultation workshop.
-ILO supported VCCI to develop the guideline on Business Continuity Plan and conduct two training workshops on Socially Responsible Labour Practices for a safe workplace, resilience, and business continuity organized for supporting the Business Coalition on CSR- led by VCCI in the Northern and the Southern areas
-ILO provided technical support for organizing two CSR pilot training courses organized for 110 final-year students of faculties of seafood processing and fisheries management at Nha Trang University  (NTU)- Central Coastal region;
-ILO supported for CSR training module adapted for fishery and seafood training of NTU.
-ILO supported for 25 lecturers of NTU participated in e-learning course on ILS of ITC ILO
-ILO organized a CSR topic seminar for 100 final-year students of faculties of law, economic, agriculture, and seafood processing at Can Tho University (CTU)-Mekong Delta.
-ILO organized a CSR Training Of Trainers (TOT)  at CTU for 25 lecturers from 13 universities and institutes in Mekong Delta</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">4.4.1. Number of member States with policies or measures to promote alignment of business practices with decent work priorities and a human-centred approach to the future of work</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.3. Increased capacity of member States to develop policies, legislation and other measures that are specifically aimed at facilitating the transition of enterprises to formality</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">- In the context of implementing and compliant the trade for sustainable development chapter of the EU-Viet Nam Free Trade Agreement, the knowledge and capacity of key actors of the supply chain of seafood and wood processing industry, on the compliance with the 2019 labour code was enhanced; the code complies with the ILO Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at work as well as the ILO MNE Declaration;
-The national resourced trainers were available for spreading and replicating the socially responsible labour practices training, in particular, the extensions to the Viet Nam Corporative Alliance (VCA) system, which helps to improve the knowledge and skills for speeding up the formalization, trade, and market development for diversifying its services to VCA members
 - ILO organized a policy dialogue between actors/ stakeholders of seafood and wood processing sectors on Corporate Social Responsibility/Responsible Business Conduct, including dissemination of two studies on CSR practices in these sectors. 
- ILO provided technical and financial support for organizing two CSR training of trainers for 41 trainers of VCA from 11 provinces of Mekong Delta and from 13 Northern mountainous provinces who further organised CSR training activities for their members and network;
- The awareness of the VCA members was raised on the importance of the &quot;Up-skill&quot; national strategy to the cooperatives network as well as the important bridging role of the cooperative system in the formalization process was highlighted. ILO supported VCA organizing the stakeholder consultation workshop on &quot;Upskill&quot; for the Viet Nam Cooperative Alliance network</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">4.3.1. Number of member States that have put in place measures that aim to facilitate the transition of enterprises and the workers they employ to formality</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
